 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of rays $n$ and the number of candidates per ray $m$. Specifically $O(n \cdot m)$.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of rays and candidates to store intermediate loss values during accumulation.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources. [G]
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources. [G]
 *   **Strengths:** Promotes binary occupancy values, avoiding the blended, non-binary surfaces common in standard volumetric alpha-compositing [S1]. It provides a principled theoretical foundation for surface selection without adding complex hyperparameters for penalty terms [S1].
 *   **Limitations:** Requires optimization of candidate colors $\mathbf{L}_{\mathbf{p}}$ across multiple viewpoints to resolve ambiguity if colors are not fixed [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When distinct surface geometry is desired and standard alpha-compositing leads to translucent or blended artifacts.
-*   **When not to use:** When volumetric effects (transparency, fog) are explicitly desired and should be preserved rather than forced into binary surfaces.
